Do not ignore the s match number when the regex starts with ^.
The s flag parser skipped a match number N when the regex began with
^, so the first match was replaced instead of the Nth: "s/^a/X/2"
turned "aaa" into "Xaa", where GNU sed leaves the line alone because ^
matches only once, and in the extended regex "^a|b", which can match
many times, "s/^a|b/X/2" replaced the leading a instead of the first b.
Parse the number whatever the regex; the matching loop already keeps
^ from matching after the start. But when the first match was an
empty one at the end of the line and the loop skipped it, it did not
note that the end had been tried, and counted a second match there:
"s/^/X/2" still changed an empty line to "X", and "s/$/X/2" turned
"abc" into "abcX". Note it.

---
 editors/sed.c |   21 ++++++++++++---------
 1 file changed, 12 insertions(+), 9 deletions(-)

--- a/editors/sed.c
+++ b/editors/sed.c
@@ -475,13 +475,11 @@
 		dbg("s flag:'%c'", substr[idx]);
 		/* Parse match number */
 		if (isdigit(substr[idx])) {
-			if (match[0] != '^') {
-				/* Match 0 treated as all, multiple matches we take the last one. */
-				const char *pos = substr + idx;
+			/* Match 0 treated as all, multiple matches we take the last one. */
+			const char *pos = substr + idx;
 /* FIXME: error check? */
-				sed_cmd->which_match = (unsigned)strtol(substr+idx, (char**) &pos, 10);
-				idx = pos - substr - 1;
-			}
+			sed_cmd->which_match = (unsigned)strtol(substr+idx, (char**) &pos, 10);
+			idx = pos - substr - 1;
 			continue;
 		}
 		/* Skip spaces, but not a newline, which ends the command */
@@ -916,9 +914,14 @@
 		) {
 			for (i = 0; i < end; i++)
 				pipe_putc(*line++);
-			/* Null match? Print one more char */
-			if (start == end && *line)
-				pipe_putc(*line++);
+			/* Null match? Print one more char, or if at the
+			 * end, note that we tried matching there */
+			if (start == end) {
+				if (*line)
+					pipe_putc(*line++);
+				else
+					tried_at_eol = 1;
+			}
 			goto next;
 		}
 
